# Grade 12 (part d): teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'12-10-1': dict(
  saint="Jacques Fesch: a murderer who converted in prison and went to the guillotine with faith.",
  tryit=["Lay out the Church’s teaching on the death penalty.", "State the strongest arguments on each side.", "Write what mercy and justice each require."],
  saintStory="Jacques Fesch was born in Paris in 1930, the son of a wealthy Belgian banker. He grew up in a household without warmth, left school early, and drifted. He married and had a daughter, but he abandoned his family because he wanted to sail to the South Pacific to find freedom. To pay for the boat, he planned a robbery of a currency exchange office in Paris on February 25, 1954. In the attempt, he shot and killed a police officer, Jean Vergnes, a husband and father. He was arrested, tried and sentenced to death.\nIn prison, he was an atheist, but his lawyer’s encouragement moved him. In 1954 he described a night of overwhelming experience in which he felt himself cry out for help and be seized by a hand. He began to read the Bible and to pray. He wrote many letters, later published as Light Over the Scaffold, speaking of peace and sorrow.\nHe was guillotined on October 1, 1957, at age twenty-seven. On the morning of his death he wrote in his notebook, “In five hours I will see Jesus!” Cardinal Lustiger of Paris opened his cause for canonization. His case shows both the gravity of his crime and the possibility of conversion. We pray also for the family of the officer he killed.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say carefully, “This lesson involves violence and killing. We keep in mind victims and their families.” Ask, “What is punishment for? Revenge, protection, deterrence, or rehabilitation?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Catechism on the death penalty. Traditionally, the Church recognized that public authority could, in cases of extreme gravity, impose the death penalty if it was the only practical way to defend human life against an unjust aggressor. But in 2018 Pope Francis revised paragraph 2267: ‘Recourse to the death penalty on the part of legitimate authority, following a fair trial, was long considered an appropriate response to the gravity of certain crimes… Today, however, there is an increasing awareness that the dignity of the person is not lost even after the commission of very serious crimes… Consequently, the Church teaches, in the light of the Gospel, that ‘the death penalty is inadmissible because it is an attack on the inviolability and dignity of the person,’ and she works with determination for its abolition worldwide.’ This is a development of doctrine, building on John Paul II’s Evangelium Vitae 56, which said that cases where execution is absolutely necessary ‘are very rare, if not practically non-existent.’”", "Say: “Reasons given: modern prisons can protect society, making execution unnecessary; the risk of executing the innocent; unequal application by race and class; the dignity of every person, even the guilty; the chance for repentance; and that executions perpetuate violence. Objections: the demands of justice for the victims; deterrence; and that the Church historically accepted it. The Church replies that justice can be served by life imprisonment; the penalty should aim at the good of the offender and the community; mercy doesn’t cancel justice; and the Church has always sought the repentance of the guilty. Victims: the Church stands with victims and families, and recognizes their pain and need for justice. Many families find that execution doesn’t heal. Restorative justice approaches can help.”", "Say: “War and peace (see Grade 12 unit 3). Just war criteria and modern war. The Catechism affirms legitimate defense. The Church condemns the targeting of civilians and weapons of mass destruction. Conscientious objection is honored. Issues: drones, cyber war, torture (never allowed), humanitarian intervention, refugees. Peacemaking: dialogue, justice and forgiveness. ‘The fruit of justice will be peace.’ (Isaiah 32:17) Jacques Fesch’s story raises a question: what do we owe those who have done terrible things? The Church answers: justice and mercy, protection of society, and hope for conversion. We also ask: do we see the dignity in every person? That includes victims first. And finally, there’s the question of how we respond to wrongs we ourselves suffer: forgiveness (Matthew 18:21-22; Luke 23:34). Forgiveness is not forgetting or excusing; it is choosing not to hate.”"],
    ask=["What does the Catechism now say of the death penalty? (It is inadmissible because it attacks the dignity of the person.)", "What did John Paul II say about necessity? (Cases are rare, if not practically non-existent.)", "What do we owe victims? (Justice, support and compassion.)"],
    practice=["Make a two-column chart: arguments for the death penalty and arguments against.", "Add the Church’s response to each.", "Write a paragraph defending the Church’s position respectfully to a friend who disagrees.", "Write a prayer for victims and for the conversion of offenders."],
    close="Pray for victims and their families and for prisoners. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for someone in prison and for the families they harmed.”")),

'12-10-2': dict(
  saint="Joachim and Anne: according to tradition, an older couple without children who prayed and were heard.",
  tryit=["Explain why the Church objects to IVF and cloning.", "Explain what it supports for infertile couples.", "Write a compassionate reply."],
  saintStory="The Bible doesn’t name Mary’s parents. Their names, Joachim and Anne, come from an early Christian text, the Protoevangelium of James, from the second century. It is not Scripture, but the Church has honored these two as saints since ancient times, on July 26.\nAccording to the story, Joachim was a wealthy and devout man whose offering at the Temple was rejected because he had no children. Humiliated, he went into the desert to fast and pray for forty days. Anne wept in her garden, lamenting that she was childless, comparing herself to the birds that have nests. An angel appeared to each, promising a child.\nThey conceived and named her Mary. When she was three, they brought her to the Temple, as they had vowed. This tradition echoes Hannah, mother of Samuel, and Sarah and Elizabeth, whose children were gifts after long waiting. For couples who struggle with infertility, Joachim and Anne are a patron.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say gently, “Many couples long for children and cannot have them. Our teaching must be firm and compassionate. Some here may have family who struggled.” Ask, “What do you think a couple longing for a child should be able to do? Are there limits?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church honors the desire for children as a good, and calls infertility a cross. Donum Vitae (1987) and Dignitas Personae (2008) explain the principles. First: a child is a gift, not a right or a product. Second: the child should be conceived in the marital act, as an expression of the spouses’ love, not made in a lab. The unitive and procreative meanings are united. So IVF (in vitro fertilization), where eggs are fertilized outside the body, is morally wrong in the Church’s teaching: it separates procreation from the conjugal act, often involves the creation of more embryos than are implanted, some of which are frozen or destroyed, and treats the child as a product. Surrogacy raises similar problems, and exploits women. Artificial insemination by a donor violates the marriage covenant. Cloning for reproduction or research is gravely wrong.”", "Say: “What the Church supports: treatment that helps the marital act achieve its end, e.g., medical treatment of the causes of infertility such as hormone therapy, surgery for blocked tubes, treating endometriosis. NaProTechnology and Natural Family Planning methods to track fertility. Adoption and foster care: generous ways of parenthood. Spiritual fruitfulness: service, teaching, mentoring, godparenting. The Church recognizes that these teachings are painful for couples. A child conceived by IVF is a person with the same dignity: the child is not to blame. Pastoral care should be tender. Also, regarding embryos already frozen: a moral dilemma; the Church urges that they be respected, and the idea of ‘adoption’ of embryos remains debated.”", "Say: “Related bioethics issues. Prenatal testing: acceptable for care, wrong if used to eliminate. Preimplantation genetic diagnosis: involves discarding embryos. Contraception and sterilization (see Grade 10). Gene editing: therapy is acceptable; enhancement and germline editing raise grave concerns. Stem cells (see above). Research on human subjects: informed consent, no harm. Organ donation: a gift. AI and genetics. How to discuss: with charity and clarity. The principle: ‘Is this respectful of the dignity of the person at every stage? Does it treat the person as a gift or a product?’ Dignitas Personae: ‘The Church… offers comfort and encouragement to couples who struggle with infertility.’ Joachim and Anne: faithful waiting; God’s gift in his time.”"],
    ask=["Why does the Church oppose IVF? (It separates procreation from the marital act and often destroys embryos.)", "What does the Church support for infertile couples? (Treating causes, NaPro methods, adoption, spiritual fruitfulness.)", "Does a child conceived by IVF have full dignity? (Yes.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart: Methods the Church accepts / Methods she doesn’t / Why.", "Write a compassionate response to a friend whose cousin is using IVF.", "List ways to support couples who struggle with infertility.", "Compose a prayer for such couples."],
    close="Pray for couples longing for children and for children awaiting adoption. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Reach out kindly to someone who is struggling.”")),

'12-10-3': dict(
  saint="The Magi: foreigners who followed a star to Bethlehem.",
  tryit=["Sort Church statements about salvation.", "Mark what is certain and what is hope.", "Write your own careful statement."],
  saintStory="Matthew’s Gospel tells that after Jesus was born in Bethlehem, magi from the East came to Jerusalem, asking, “Where is he who has been born king of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the East, and have come to worship him.” They were probably learned men, astronomers or priests of a Persian or Babylonian tradition. They were not Jews. They had no Scripture; they had the sky, and a longing for truth.\nKing Herod and the chief priests, who knew the Scriptures, told them: Bethlehem. The magi followed the star and found the child with Mary his mother. They knelt and worshiped him, and offered gifts of gold, frankincense and myrrh. Warned in a dream not to return to Herod, they went home by another way.\nThe Church celebrates this on Epiphany. The Fathers saw the magi as the first fruits of the Gentiles, a sign that Christ came for all nations. They sought sincerely, and were led by what light they had to the one true Light. Tradition names them Caspar, Melchior and Balthasar.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What happens to a person who lived a good life but never heard of Jesus? What do you think the Church teaches? What do you hope?” Take honest answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church holds two truths together. One: Christ is the only Savior; ‘there is no other name under heaven given among men by which we must be saved’ (Acts 4:12), and the Church is necessary for salvation (CCC 846). Two: God ‘desires all men to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth’ (1 Timothy 2:4). How do these fit? Lumen Gentium 14-16 and the Catechism 836-848: Those fully incorporated into the Church have the means of salvation in fullness. Baptized non-Catholics are in imperfect communion and have access to grace. Jews: the covenant remains. Muslims, with us, adore one God. Those who ‘through no fault of their own do not know the Gospel of Christ or his Church, but sincerely seek God and, moved by grace, try in their actions to do his will as they know it through the dictates of their conscience—those too may achieve eternal salvation.’ (LG 16)”", "Say: “So the answer to ‘Can non-Catholics be saved?’ is yes, through Christ and in some way related to the Church, even if they don’t know it. This is not indifferentism (‘all religions are equal’) nor ‘anonymous Christianity’ as such. The Church rejects any idea that one can reject Christ and the Church knowingly and be saved (LG 14). Also, the Church doesn’t deny the possibility of salvation for non-Christians. Their salvation is by Christ’s grace. Also related: baptism of desire and blood; limbo (a theory not taught as doctrine; the 2007 International Theological Commission document gives grounds for hope for unbaptized infants). Also: Pope Pius IX’s ‘invincible ignorance.’ Francis Xavier’s sorrow over unbaptized persons contrasts with the clearer teaching of Vatican II.”", "Say: “What difference does it make? If people can be saved without knowing the Church, why evangelize? The Church’s answer: (1) Christ commanded it; (2) the Gospel is the fullness of truth and a gift that everyone deserves; (3) the Church provides the sacraments, community and clarity; (4) we don’t know the fate of any individual, so we have urgency; (5) evangelization is love: we want others to know the one who loves them. Paul: ‘Woe to me if I do not preach the gospel!’ (1 Corinthians 9:16) We hold both: confidence in God’s mercy and urgency of mission. The magi show that God leads people by many ways to Christ, using their own gifts and questions. Be careful to avoid two errors: ‘only Catholics are saved’ and ‘it doesn’t matter what you believe.’ Hope: the Church prays for the salvation of all.”"],
    ask=["Can non-Catholics be saved? (Yes, through Christ, if they sincerely seek God and follow conscience through no fault of their own.)", "Why evangelize if so? (Christ commanded it; the Gospel is a gift; the Church provides the fullness of the means.)", "What two errors should we avoid? (Only Catholics are saved; it doesn’t matter what you believe.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart of groups: baptized Catholics, other Christians, Jews, Muslims, those who never heard of Christ, those who reject the Church knowingly.", "Write what the Church teaches about each, with Lumen Gentium references.", "Write a careful two-sentence statement on salvation for a friend.", "List three ways to evangelize without arguing."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, you desire all to be saved. Make us instruments of your mercy.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Explain this teaching to someone.”")),

'12-11-1': dict(
  saint="Ivo of Kermartin: a judge who defended the poor without a fee.",
  tryit=["Analyze three actions by object, intention and circumstances.", "Identify an intrinsically evil act.", "Explain proportionalism’s error."],
  saintStory="Ivo Hélory was born around 1253 in Brittany, France, into a noble family. He studied law and theology in Paris and Orléans. Returning to Brittany, he was appointed an ecclesiastical judge in Rennes, then in Tréguier. He became known for his fairness and incorruptibility. He refused bribes, listened to every case, and tried to reconcile the parties before judgment. He is said to have often paid the legal costs of poor litigants and represented them for free, earning the title “advocate of the poor.”\nHe was ordained a priest in 1284 and served as a parish pastor while continuing as a judge. He gave away his income, wore plain clothes and fasted. He visited prisoners and cared for the sick, orphans and beggars. He died in 1303 and was canonized in 1347.\nHe is the patron of lawyers and judges. There is an old saying in Brittany about him: “Sanctus Yvo erat Brito, advocatus et non latro, res miranda populo” (“St. Ivo was a Breton, a lawyer and not a thief, a thing to marvel at”).",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “A person lies to protect an innocent friend. Another person lies to cheat on a test. Both told a lie. Are the two acts morally the same? What differs?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Catechism (CCC 1749-1761) teaches that the morality of human acts depends on three sources. The object: the act itself, what is chosen, ‘the proximate end of a deliberate decision.’ The intention: ‘the end for which we act,’ which lies in the acting person’s will. The circumstances: the who, what, where, when, how, and the consequences. ‘A morally good act requires the goodness of the object, of the end, and of the circumstances together.’ If the object is evil, the act is evil, regardless of the intention. ‘The end does not justify the means.’ A good intention (e.g., giving to the poor out of vanity) does not make an evil act good, and a bad intention spoils a good act (e.g., giving alms to be praised; Matthew 6:1-4).”", "Say: “Intrinsically evil acts: acts which, by their object, are always wrong, regardless of intention or circumstances. Examples: murder of the innocent, adultery, rape, torture, lying, blasphemy, direct abortion, euthanasia. Veritatis Splendor (1993), by John Paul II, defends this against proportionalism and consequentialism, which hold that an act’s morality depends on the proportion of good and bad results. The Church answers: no; a person may never do evil that good may come of it (Romans 3:8). Circumstances can increase or reduce guilt, but they cannot change an act’s species. Reduced responsibility: ignorance, fear, force, habit. Double effect: when an act has both a good and an evil effect, it may be permissible if (1) the act itself is good or neutral, (2) the good effect isn’t obtained by the evil effect, (3) the evil is not intended, and (4) there is a proportionately grave reason.”", "Say: “Passions. Emotions aren’t good or bad in themselves; they become morally good or evil as they are governed by reason and will. Virtue directs the passions. A judge like Ivo must weigh facts, law and mercy. Some practical use: when a moral decision is hard, ask three questions. What am I doing, exactly? Why am I doing it? What are the circumstances and likely consequences? Then check with the Church’s teaching. Moral absolutes protect the weak. Pope John Paul II: ‘The Church… does not hold that conscience can create moral truth.’ Ivo’s example: he used his skill for justice and mercy, and refused the temptation to make exceptions for gain. Many modern debates turn on this question: whether results alone decide.”"],
    ask=["What are the three sources of morality? (Object, intention, circumstances.)", "Can a good intention justify an evil act? (No.)", "What is proportionalism? (The theory that morality depends only on the proportion of good and bad results; the Church rejects it.)"],
    practice=["Analyze three cases by object, intention and circumstances: a nurse lies to protect a patient from an abuser; a student cheats to keep a scholarship; a doctor prescribes strong pain medication to a dying patient.", "Identify the object, the intention and the circumstances of each.", "Decide if any act is intrinsically evil.", "Apply double effect where relevant."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, give me clear sight and a just heart.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Analyze a decision you face by the three sources.”")),

'12-11-2': dict(
  saint="Raymond of Peñafort: a lawyer who organized the Church’s laws into one book.",
  tryit=["Distinguish eternal, natural, Old, New and civil law.", "Give an example of each.", "Explain what makes a law unjust."],
  saintStory="Raymond was born around 1175 in Catalonia, Spain. He studied canon and civil law at Bologna and became a professor. In 1222 he joined the Dominicans, and soon after Pope Gregory IX called him to Rome. Gregory asked him to organize the many scattered decrees of popes and councils, which contradicted one another and made it hard for judges and bishops. Raymond spent about three years on this work, and in 1234, the Decretals of Gregory IX was published. It became the main law book of the Church for nearly 700 years, until the Code of Canon Law of 1917.\nRaymond also wrote a handbook for confessors, the Summa de casibus poenitentiae, and helped found the Mercedarians, an order that rescued Christian captives. He was elected Master General of the Dominicans in 1238 but resigned after two years because of his health. He is said to have urged missionaries to learn Arabic and Hebrew.\nHe died in 1275, aged about a hundred. He was canonized in 1601 and is patron of canon lawyers.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Why do we have laws? Is a law just because it’s the law? What would you say to someone who says, ‘I obeyed the law’ when the law was unjust?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Aquinas, in the Summa, defines law as ‘an ordinance of reason for the common good, made by one who has care of the community, and promulgated.’ He distinguishes four kinds. Eternal law: God’s wisdom guiding all creation. Natural law: the human participation in the eternal law, known by reason, written on the heart (Romans 2:14-15). It commands to do good and avoid evil, preserve life, seek truth, live in society. It is universal and immutable. Human (positive) law: laws made by legitimate authority, applying natural law to circumstances. Divine law: revealed law, the Old Law and the New Law. The Catechism (1950-1986) presents this.”", "Say: “The Old Law: the law of Moses, the Ten Commandments. ‘Holy, just and good’ (Romans 7:12) but imperfect: it points to Christ but cannot give the grace to fulfill it. It is a preparation for the Gospel, a tutor (Galatians 3:24). Its moral precepts remain; ceremonial and civil precepts are fulfilled in Christ. The New Law: the law of the Gospel, ‘the grace of the Holy Spirit received by faith in Christ’ (Aquinas). It is law of love, of grace and of freedom. The Sermon on the Mount, the Beatitudes, the Lord’s Prayer. It does not abolish the Old but fulfills it, adding the counsels (e.g., poverty, chastity, obedience). Its power: the Holy Spirit within.”", "Say: “Unjust laws. Aquinas and Augustine: ‘an unjust law is no law at all’ in the sense that it has no binding moral force, though one may sometimes comply to avoid greater harm. Martin Luther King Jr., in his Letter from Birmingham Jail, cited Aquinas: ‘An unjust law is a human law that is not rooted in eternal law and natural law.’ The Church teaches that citizens have a duty of conscience not to follow directives that contradict the moral order (CCC 2242). ‘We must obey God rather than men.’ (Acts 5:29) Conscientious objection. Civil disobedience, with a nonviolent spirit, is sometimes justified. Canon law: the Church’s own law, which is human law ordered to the salvation of souls; ‘the supreme law is the salvation of souls.’ Raymond’s service shows that law at its best serves justice and charity.”"],
    ask=["What are the four kinds of law? (Eternal, natural, human, divine.)", "What is the New Law? (The grace of the Holy Spirit, the law of love and freedom.)", "How should we respond to unjust laws? (Follow conscience, nonviolently, obeying God rather than men.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart with five laws: eternal, natural, Old, New, civil. Give an example of each.", "Choose one civil law and analyze whether it is just by natural law.", "Read a paragraph of King’s Letter from Birmingham Jail.", "Write a short paragraph on when disobedience is justified."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, write your law on our hearts.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read the Letter from Birmingham Jail.”")),

'12-11-3': dict(
  saint="Caesarius of Arles: a bishop whose council at Orange taught that even the beginning of faith is a gift.",
  tryit=["Lay out the Catholic account of justification.", "Contrast it with Pelagian and Reformation views.", "Explain merit."],
  saintStory="Caesarius was born around 470 in Gaul, to a Christian family. As a teenager he joined the monastery on the island of Lérins, but his health failed and he went to Arles. In 502, at about thirty-two, he was made bishop of Arles, a position he held for forty years. He was a pastor, caring for the poor, redeeming captives and building hospitals. He wrote a rule for nuns, one of the first for women in Gaul, which his sister Caesaria followed as abbess.\nHe preached simple sermons in the vernacular, designed to be understood by ordinary people, and urged them to attend Mass, pray and give alms. In 529, he presided over the Council of Orange, which settled a dispute about grace. Some, called semi-Pelagians, taught that people could take the first step toward God on their own, and that grace followed. The council, with the pope’s approval, taught that even the beginning of faith is a gift of grace.\nCaesarius died in 542. The decisions of Orange were later used at the Council of Trent.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If someone gives you a gift, you can accept it or refuse it. If a drowning person is thrown a rope, he can grab it. But who made the rope, threw it and gave strength to grab it? Where does our part start?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Justification is the work of God’s grace that makes us righteous. The Catechism (1987-2029): ‘Justification is not only the remission of sins, but also the sanctification and renewal of the interior man.’ (Trent) It is the most excellent work of God’s mercy. It comes through faith in Jesus Christ and Baptism. It conforms us to the righteousness of God, who makes us inwardly just. It is God’s initiative: ‘the first work of the grace of the Holy Spirit is conversion.’ We can’t earn it. Paul: ‘For by grace you have been saved through faith… not because of works, lest any man should boast.’ (Ephesians 2:8-9) Yet ‘faith working through love’ (Galatians 5:6). Trent: we cooperate freely with grace, which doesn’t destroy freedom.”", "Say: “Errors. Pelagianism (Pelagius, c. 400): humans can achieve salvation by their own efforts; grace is merely help; denies original sin. Condemned; Augustine opposed it. Semi-Pelagianism: we can take the first step; condemned at Orange 529. Reformation views: Luther held that we are justified by faith alone, righteousness is imputed (credited) externally, and good works are fruits but do not contribute. Calvin added predestination. Catholic response at Trent (1547): justification is real interior renewal, by grace; faith is the beginning, but it must be formed by charity; good works are meritorious by grace; one can lose justification through mortal sin and regain it through Penance. The 1999 Joint Declaration with Lutherans found a consensus: ‘By grace alone, in faith in Christ’s saving work and not because of any merit on our part, we are accepted by God and receive the Holy Spirit.’”", "Say: “Merit. The Church teaches that we can merit eternal life and an increase of grace, but only because of God’s grace and the merits of Christ. ‘When God crowns our merits, he crowns his own gifts.’ (Augustine) Merit is not a claim on God by justice, but a gift God gives, because he wills to associate us in his work. Thérèse of Lisieux prayed: ‘I wish to be clothed in your own justice and to receive from your love the eternal possession of yourself.’ Perseverance: a gift. Assurance: we hope for salvation but cannot be certain of final perseverance without special revelation; we trust in God’s mercy. Predestination: God wills all to be saved; the Church does not teach that anyone is predestined to hell. Practical: we pray, ‘Lord, I can’t without you’ and also ‘I will cooperate.’ Grace and freedom are not rivals.”"],
    ask=["What is justification? (God’s work of making us righteous inwardly, by grace.)", "What did the Council of Orange teach? (Even the beginning of faith is a gift of grace.)", "What is merit? (God rewards what his grace enables us to do.)"],
    practice=["Make a three-column chart: Pelagian / Reformation / Catholic account of justification.", "Fill in on what causes our salvation, the role of works and assurance.", "Write a plain-language explanation of Catholic teaching for a Protestant friend.", "Share and check with a partner."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, without you I can do nothing; with you, all things.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read Ephesians 2:1-10.”")),

'12-12-1': dict(
  saint="Pius V: a Dominican pope who asked all Christendom to pray the Rosary before the Battle of Lepanto.",
  tryit=["Pray a psalm in the Church’s way.", "Pray a decade of the Rosary with a Gospel scene.", "Write what Mary teaches about prayer."],
  saintStory="Antonio Ghislieri was born in 1504 in northern Italy, to a poor family. He worked as a shepherd as a boy, then entered the Dominicans at fourteen. He became a strict inquisitor and bishop, a man of prayer and penance. He was elected pope in 1566 as Pius V. He lived simply, still wearing his white Dominican habit, which is why popes dress in white.\nHe implemented the Council of Trent: he published the Roman Catechism (1566), the Roman Breviary (1568), and the Roman Missal (1570), which standardized the Mass for centuries. In 1571, the Ottoman Turkish fleet threatened Europe. Pius organized the Holy League of Spain, Venice and the papal states, and asked Catholics to pray the Rosary. On October 7, 1571, the Christian fleet won an unexpected victory at the Battle of Lepanto. Pius reportedly knew of it before the news arrived. He attributed the victory to Our Lady and instituted the feast of Our Lady of Victory, now Our Lady of the Rosary, on October 7.\nHe died in 1572 and was canonized in 1712.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What prayers do you know by heart? Which ones do you actually pray? Why do you think the Church gave us ready-made prayers like the Psalms and the Rosary?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Psalms are the prayer book of the Church. Jesus prayed them: at the Last Supper they sang the Hallel (Psalms 113-118), and on the cross he prayed Psalm 22. The Church prays them daily in the Liturgy of the Hours and at every Mass. Athanasius said that most of Scripture speaks to us, but the Psalms speak for us: ‘the Psalter has this marvel: it contains all the movements of our souls.’ Types: praise, thanksgiving, trust, lament, repentance, royal psalms (fulfilled in Christ), wisdom. Praying them: read slowly, enter into the situation, hear Christ praying, make it your own. The Church ends each psalm with the Gloria Patri, giving them a Christian sense.”", "Say: “Mary at prayer. Luke shows her receptive: ‘Let it be to me according to your word’; the Magnificat, composed of Old Testament echoes (Hannah’s song); she ‘kept all these things, pondering them in her heart’; at Cana she intercedes; at the cross she stands; in the upper room she prays with the apostles. The Catechism (2617-2619) calls her the model of prayer. The Hail Mary: the angel’s greeting, Elizabeth’s greeting, and the Church’s petition. The Rosary: a Gospel in miniature. John Paul II in Rosarium Virginis Mariae (2002): it is ‘a compendium of the Gospel’; ‘to recite the Rosary is nothing other than to contemplate with Mary the face of Christ.’ He added the Luminous Mysteries. How to pray it: begin with the Sign of the Cross and Creed; each decade: announce the mystery, Our Father, ten Hail Marys, Glory Be, and the Fatima prayer; end with the Hail, Holy Queen.”", "Say: “Other Marian prayers: the Angelus, Regina Caeli, Memorare, Salve Regina, the Litany of Loreto, the Magnificat, Marian consecration (Montfort). Understand the theology: we pray ‘to’ Mary in the sense of asking her to pray for us: ‘pray for us sinners, now and at the hour of our death.’ Mary leads to Jesus: ‘Do whatever he tells you.’ The Rosary’s fruits: contemplation, peace, intercession. Pius V and Lepanto: Catholics believed in the power of the Rosary for intercession. Misunderstandings: Rosary as repetition: Jesus warns against babbling (Matthew 6:7), but repetition with attention can quiet the mind, as a mantra of love. A practical start: one decade a day. Use beads, an app or fingers. Don’t worry about distractions.”"],
    ask=["Why do we pray the Psalms? (Jesus prayed them; the Church prays them; they cover every feeling.)", "What did John Paul II call the Rosary? (A compendium of the Gospel.)", "What happened at Lepanto? (The Christian fleet defeated the Ottoman fleet in 1571 after the Rosary was prayed.)"],
    practice=["Pray Psalm 23 slowly in the first person, then in the voice of Christ.", "Pray one decade of the Rosary together (e.g., the Annunciation), pausing to picture the scene at each Hail Mary.", "Write three things Mary teaches about prayer.", "Choose a daily rhythm: one psalm and one decade."],
    close="Pray the Hail, Holy Queen. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray a decade daily this week.”")),

'12-12-2': dict(
  saint="Seraphim of Sarov: a Russian monk whose greeting to every visitor was, “My joy, Christ is risen!”",
  tryit=["Compare three schools of spirituality.", "Choose one to try for a month.", "Write what it adds to your prayer."],
  saintStory="Seraphim of Sarov was born Prokhor Moshnin in 1754 in Kursk, Russia, to a merchant family. As a boy he fell from a bell tower and was said to be miraculously unhurt; he fell gravely ill, and had a vision of Mary, who healed him. At nineteen he entered the monastery of Sarov. He became a hermit in a cabin in the forest. For a thousand days and nights he knelt on a rock, praying the Jesus Prayer with his hands raised. In 1804, robbers beat him nearly to death, leaving him with a stoop, and he forgave them.\nAfter years of silence, he began to receive visitors, in obedience to a vision. Thousands came for advice. He greeted everyone, rich or poor, with a bow and the Easter greeting, “My joy, Christ is risen!” and called them “my joy.” He fed the bears in the forest from his hand.\nHe taught: “Acquire the spirit of peace, and thousands around you will be saved.” He died in 1833, kneeling in front of an icon. He is among the most beloved Russian Orthodox saints; Catholics also honor the holiness of the Eastern saints.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If you wanted to learn to play an instrument, you wouldn’t invent it from scratch. You’d find a teacher. Are there schools of prayer? Whom would you choose to learn from?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church has many schools of spirituality, each a way of following Christ, shaped by a saint. They don’t compete but complement. Benedictine: ‘Ora et labora.’ Liturgical prayer, the Rule, stability, hospitality, lectio divina. Franciscan: poverty, joy, love of creation, the humanity of Christ (the crèche, the cross). Dominican: study, preaching, truth, the Rosary. Carmelite: contemplative prayer, the interior life (Teresa, John of the Cross, Thérèse). Ignatian: finding God in all things, discernment, the Examen, the Spiritual Exercises, ‘for the greater glory of God.’ Salesian (Francis de Sales): holiness for all, gentleness, devotion in ordinary life. Vincentian: service of the poor. Marian (Montfort): consecration to Jesus through Mary. Eastern: the Jesus Prayer, icons, hesychasm, theosis (Seraphim).”", "Say: “Modern movements: Opus Dei (sanctify work), Focolare (unity), Communion and Liberation, Neocatechumenal Way, Schoenstatt, Taizé (simple chant, silence), Charismatic Renewal, Catholic Worker (Dorothy Day: service and peace), Sant’Egidio, L’Arche (living with people with disabilities), the Little Way of Thérèse. Each has a charism: a gift of the Spirit for the Church. How do we choose? We don’t have to choose just one. The question is: which helps me to know, love and follow Christ? Practical: read the biography of a saint; try their method; ask a spiritual director; join a group. Spiritual direction: a regular conversation with a wise person to help discern God’s work in your life.”", "Say: “Spiritual reading and life. Read the saints: Confessions (Augustine), Story of a Soul (Thérèse), The Interior Castle (Teresa), Introduction to the Devout Life (Francis de Sales), Imitation of Christ, The Way of a Pilgrim (Eastern), Abandonment to Divine Providence (de Caussade), Divine Mercy in My Soul (Faustina). Guard against distortions: consumer spirituality, emotionalism, individualism. The test: it leads to humility, love of Christ, the Church and neighbor. Seraphim: his peace and joy flowed from the Jesus Prayer and years of silence. His example prompts us to find a deeper interior life. ‘Acquire the spirit of peace and thousands around you will be saved.’ Peace is mission.”"],
    ask=["Name two schools of spirituality and their emphasis. (Ignatian: finding God in all things; Carmelite: contemplation.)", "What is spiritual direction? (Regular guidance to discern God’s work in your life.)", "What is the test of a spirituality? (Whether it leads to humility, love of Christ, the Church and neighbor.)"],
    practice=["Make a table of five schools: founder, emphasis, practice, one book.", "Choose one practice to try for a month (Examen, lectio, Jesus Prayer, the Little Way, Rosary).", "Plan when and how you will do it.", "Choose a book to read."],
    close="Pray the Jesus Prayer together slowly. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Greet someone today with joy, as Seraphim did.”")),

'12-12-3': dict(
  saint="Jane de Chantal: a widow and foundress who prayed through forty years of darkness.",
  tryit=["List your obstacles to prayer.", "Pair each with a remedy.", "Make a plan for the next dry season."],
  saintStory="Jane Frances Frémyot was born in 1572 in Dijon, France. She married Baron Christophe de Chantal and had six children, four of whom survived. She ran their estate with skill and generosity. In 1601, her husband was killed in a hunting accident. Jane, at twenty-eight, was devastated. She forgave the man who accidentally shot him, and agreed to be godmother to his child.\nIn 1604, she met Francis de Sales, bishop of Geneva, who became her spiritual director. Over six years, they developed a plan for a new community for women, the Visitation, who would be able to enter with weaker health or older age than the severe orders. She provided for her children and in 1610 founded the Visitation at Annecy. It grew to eighty houses in her lifetime.\nFor almost forty years, Jane experienced great inner darkness: dryness, doubts and a sense of God’s absence, even as she led her sisters. Francis’s letters guided her. She persevered with simple fidelity, praying even when she felt nothing. She died in 1641 and was canonized in 1767.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “When you pray, what gets in the way? Tiredness, boredom, doubt, distraction, busyness, feeling nothing? Which is yours?” Take honest answers. List them.",
    teach=["Say: “The Catechism (2725-2745) lists obstacles to prayer and ways to meet them. Distractions: the commonest. Remedy: return gently to God; offer them; don’t fight. Dryness: a feeling that God is absent; may be a test or purification. Remedy: persevere in fidelity; ‘prayer is a battle’ (CCC 2725). Acedia (spiritual sloth): ‘a form of depression due to lax ascetical practice, decreasing vigilance, carelessness of heart.’ Remedy: determination. Discouragement: failure to pray, thinking you’re no good at it. Remedy: humility; God is patient. Busyness: no time. Remedy: schedule it; prayer is not an extra. Doubt: ‘Is anyone listening?’ Remedy: faith; ask for faith; the Psalms are honest. Unanswered prayer: ‘We do not know how to pray as we ought.’ (Romans 8:26) Trust the Spirit.”", "Say: “Perseverance. Jesus taught persistence: the widow and the unjust judge (Luke 18:1-8), the friend at midnight (Luke 11:5-13). ‘Pray at all times and do not lose heart.’ The dark night: John of the Cross taught that God sometimes withdraws consolations to purify love. Mother Teresa lived for decades without a sense of God’s presence, and kept serving. Jane de Chantal’s forty years of dryness. Even Jesus on the cross: ‘My God, why have you forsaken me?’ Faith is not a feeling. The measure of prayer is love, not consolation. Practical helps: a regular time and place; a simple method; a prayer partner; spiritual direction; the sacraments; the Psalms; Scripture; silence; reading the saints; short prayers during the day; fasting; being generous. Prayer is a gift to ask for.”", "Say: “A last reflection. Prayer will continue after the course ends. Make a plan for dry times: What will I do when I don’t feel like praying? Whom will I tell? What will I do for just five minutes? Francis de Sales advised Jane: ‘Do not be disturbed; simply remain faithful.’ And ‘Have patience with all things, but first of all with yourself.’ Prayer is a relationship with a loving Father. Like any relationship, it has seasons. The fruit: peace, love, service. Pray the Our Father with attention. God is faithful. ‘Be still, and know that I am God.’ (Psalm 46:10) Your course ends here, but your life of prayer is just beginning. ‘The Lord bless you and keep you.’”"],
    ask=["What is acedia? (Spiritual sloth; carelessness of heart in prayer.)", "How should we handle distractions? (Return gently to God.)", "What did Jane de Chantal do during her forty years of dryness? (She persevered in fidelity.)"],
    practice=["Make two columns: obstacles to my prayer / remedies.", "Add a plan for the next dry season: what I will do, whom I will tell, what is the minimum five-minute prayer.", "Write a short prayer to say when you feel nothing.", "Share your plan with a partner and commit to check in monthly."],
    close="Pray the Priestly Blessing: “The Lord bless you and keep you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Keep your prayer plan, especially when you don’t feel like it.”")),
}
